<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Qatar</h1><p class="st-meta">1971 CE – present · capital Doha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Independent Qatar since 1971, whose vast gas reserves made it one of the world's richest states and an outsized diplomatic and media power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> withdrew from the Gulf, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Sheikhdom of Qatar</a> chose not to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-arab-emirates">United Arab Emirates</a> and became independent on 3 September 1971. In February 1972 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa-bin-hamad-al-thani">Khalifa bin Hamad Al Thani</a> deposed his cousin Ahmad and took power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Qatar</a> is an absolute monarchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-thani">Al Thani</a> with an advisory council. The North Field, the world's largest gas field shared with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, made it a leading exporter of liquefied natural gas from the 1990s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamad-bin-khalifa-al-thani">Hamad bin Khalifa</a>, who displaced his father in 1995, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-jazeera">Al Jazeera</a> in 1996, hosted a large US air base and pursued an independent foreign policy. Most residents are foreign workers.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamim-bin-hamad-al-thani">Tamim bin Hamad</a> succeeded his father in 2013. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>, the UAE, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> blockaded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-qatar">Qatar</a> from 2017 to 2021, and in 2022 it hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">FIFA World Cup</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-qatar.jpg" alt="State of Qatar" width="320" height="126"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Qatar.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1971 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Doha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Khalifa bin Hamad Al Thani, Hamad bin Khalifa Al Thani, Tamim bin Hamad Al Thani</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/qatar">Qatar</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Qatar</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=state-of-qatar" aria-label="State of Qatar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6045 1248 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Qatar lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6365 1504L6365 1504"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6365 1504h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6365 1504h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6365 1504h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Doha. <a href="/atlas?year=1998&amp;focus=state-of-qatar">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
